   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.3",
    "topic_title": "Cellular Energy",
    "stem": "A cell must join two small molecules into a larger product. On its own, this reaction requires a net input of energy, so it does not proceed. In the cell, an enzyme first transfers a phosphate group from ATP to one reactant, forming a phosphorylated intermediate. That intermediate then reacts with the second molecule, releasing the phosphate. Which statement best explains how the cell makes the product?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Phosphate transfer from ATP couples ATP hydrolysis to the reaction, so the energy-releasing and energy-requiring steps together release net energy.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Transferring ATP's phosphate makes the phosphorylated reactant less stable and higher in energy. Its reaction with the second molecule then releases energy. Because the two processes share an intermediate, energy released by ATP hydrolysis powers the otherwise unfavorable joining step, and the coupled process as a whole releases energy."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The enzyme lowers the activation energy, which turns the energy-requiring reaction into an energy-releasing one.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses catalysis with energetics. Lowering activation energy speeds a reaction but does not change the overall energy difference between reactants and products. An energy-requiring reaction stays energy-requiring with or without an enzyme. Fix: Ask whether a factor changes only how fast a reaction goes or changes the energy balance between reactants and products."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "ATP hydrolysis releases heat that warms the reactants, giving them enough energy to combine.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats ATP as a generic energy source that works by heating. Heat released in the cell spreads out and cannot be directed to drive one specific reaction. ATP powers the reaction by transferring a phosphate group to a reactant, not by raising temperature. Fix: Trace where ATP's phosphate goes and identify which molecule it is attached to."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "ATP hydrolyzed anywhere in the cell adds energy to a shared cytoplasmic pool, and the joining reaction draws from that pool.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This pictures energy as a free-floating reservoir. Coupling requires the two processes to be linked, here through a shared phosphorylated intermediate. ATP hydrolyzed elsewhere without phosphate transfer to this reactant does not drive the reaction. Fix: Check that the stem names a shared intermediate connecting the energy-releasing and energy-requiring steps before you call them coupled."
     }
    ]
   },
