   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.3",
    "topic_title": "Cellular Energy",
    "stem": "A cell joins two small molecules to form a larger molecule. Under the cell’s conditions, this reaction requires energy input. ATP hydrolysis releases enough energy to supply that input. Which explanation best describes how ATP can support the formation of the larger molecule?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "ATP’s presence supplies the needed energy even if ATP does not undergo a reaction coupled to molecule formation.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The “ATP is energy” trap treats ATP’s presence as sufficient to power work. Energy stored in ATP must be made available through a coupled process; merely having ATP nearby does not drive molecule formation. Fix: Identify what happens to ATP and explain how that change is linked to the work."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The cell couples ATP formation to molecule formation because making ATP releases the energy needed to build the larger molecule.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The reversed-energy-flow trap confuses ATP formation with ATP hydrolysis. Forming ATP from ADP and phosphate requires energy input, so it cannot serve as the energy-releasing partner described here. Fix: Check whether the proposed ATP reaction requires energy or releases energy before assigning it a role in coupling."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "ATP hydrolysis creates new energy, which the cell uses to form the larger molecule.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The energy-creation trap mistakes energy release for energy creation. ATP hydrolysis makes energy available for coupled work, but it does not create energy; cellular processes obey conservation of energy. Fix: Trace the energy from its stored source to the cellular work instead of describing it as newly created."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The cell couples ATP hydrolysis to molecule formation, so the energy-releasing process drives the energy-requiring process.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Forming the larger molecule is the work that requires energy. Coupling that reaction to ATP hydrolysis links energy release to the work, allowing the combined process to proceed rather than leaving the energy-requiring reaction unsupported."
     }
    ]
   },
